  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Logan Webb went six scoreless, and Jung Hoo Lee hit two home runs in Arlington.",
    "what_this_means": "Webb's command was clean. Eight strikeouts on five hits, nothing conceded. That early control mattered less than what happened after: Lee's two-run blast in the second put them ahead, and his solo shot in the ninth sealed it. The rest of the lineup touched the game with six hits and three runners driven in from scoring position. They never trailed after the second inning. This was a routine win. Pitching held, offense converted chances, nobody panicked. But routine wins are the only kind that stack into streaks, and this team has not had enough of them to be comfortable yet. Webb did his part. Lee did his part. The machinery worked once.",
    "what_to_watch": "The math of staying fragile is simple: one strong game erases nothing. They are still 21 games back, still hitting .252 as a team, still winning at the rate of a .425 club. A one-run margin against Texas in August is not a breakthrough. It is a single night where everything aligned. They needed both. The starter to be clean and the bats to convert. And they got it. That is the only way this team wins games. That convergence does not happen often enough.",
